The code statements that lead to the bug are:
mod_timer(&info->timer->timer,
	  secs_to_jiffies(info->timeout) + jiffies);
Reason:
Revision 0 rules always call mod_timer() on timer->timer without checking whether the existing timer was created by revision 1 with XT_IDLETIMER_ALARM. In that case, timer->timer is uninitialized (alarm timers use different semantics), leading to debugobjects warnings and possible panic when panic_on_warn=1.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (info->timer->timer_type & XT_IDLETIMER_ALARM) {
	pr_debug("Adding/Replacing rule with same label and different timer type is not allowed\n");
	mutex_unlock(&list_mutex);
	return -EINVAL;
}
Reason:
This check rejects the rule insertion if an existing timer with the same label is of ALARM type, preventing mod_timer() from being called on an uninitialized timer_list. It ensures that revision 0 rules only reuse timers that were originally created without the alarm flag, thus avoiding the invalid timer_list access.

